r infection  because we thought we'd be able to find  this out by doing these  antibody tests but as it turns out the  situation  is a bit more complicated than that so  the serum positivity in other words how  many people have antibodies  is not a very good indication of how  many people are immune  so given that and given the  extraordinary or  quite wide heterogeneity in numbers  who have antibodies or proportions who  have antibodies  it's quite difficult to make firm  predictions  about where we're going but at the  moment  what we're seeing is not is very much in  line  with what happens to any other infection  like the coded  around this time of the year in other  words everything goes up  flu goes up uh pneumococcal pneumonia  goes up  there are a thousand people a week dying  of  flu and pneumonia at the moment so these  so the context is very important as is  an understanding a very basic  understanding of the dynamics  of these infections well you mentioned  that number dying of flu the seven day  moving average from  covert deaths is 40 at the moment which  is a lot less  but easy to to someone like me who can  do the arithmetic  that is has no medical expertise  whatsoever  i would come to the conclusion that we  were sort of false prospectus  intentionally  these figures were put to us with the  scientists being complicit  and the politicians suffering up the  ground for new lockdown measures  what would be wrong with that conclusion  well it's not  for me to say what the intention of the  scientists or  the politicians um what they are  all i can say is that i think they  should think very very carefully  about the costs of lockdown and it's not  just i think we should get away from  just talking about the economy because  people tend to separate  the economy from the impact on lives  so the economy is not just this sort of  external thing  that we have to attend to and which we  can  sort of ignore and and continue down  this kind of  narrow track of